  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "They wasted Jung Hoo Lee's go-ahead ninth and lost both games anyway.",
    "what_this_means": "Walk-offs on consecutive nights. The doubleheader was supposed to be a chance to build something. Instead, it became a clinic in how to manufacture chances and leave them on the field. In Game 1, they clawed back with two runs in the ninth and still lost 3\u20132. In Game 2, Lee sliced the go-ahead single in the ninth and they still couldn't hold it, losing 5\u20136 in extras. The lineup produced: 13 hits, two RBI from Arraez, enough traffic to have meant something. None of it stuck.",
    "what_to_watch": "This is what inconsistency looks like when the margin gets thin. They have the pieces to build an inning, but not the discipline to finish one. Lee's single should have been the turn. Instead it was just noise in a game that slipped away anyway. They've now lost three straight and sit 7.0 back in a division that doesn't wait for teams to find themselves. The formula for winning close games is simple: don't let the other team answer. They answered twice. Late-inning execution is the difference between 13-18 and a real contender. Right now they're still the first.",
